@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@
+---
+id: task-004-implement-json-merge-patch-rfc-7386-applymergepatch
+type: task
+title: "Implement JSON Merge Patch RFC 7386 applyMergePatch"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Some clients send partial updates in the simpler JSON Merge Patch format (RFC 7386) rather than
+JSON Patch (RFC 6902). Implement it, exported as `applyMergePatch(target, patch)` from
+`src/index.ts`, applying a merge patch to a target JSON document and returning the merged
+document.
+
+## What was done
+
+Added `applyMergePatch` to `src/index.ts`, implementing the recursive algorithm of RFC 7386
+section 2 (Figure 1): a `patch` that is not a JSON object replaces `target` wholesale (this also
+covers `patch: null`, which the RFC uses to mean "replace with null", as distinct from a `null`
+*member* of a patch object, which means "remove this member"); otherwise, each member of `patch` is
+merged into `target` — a `null`-valued member removes the corresponding member of `target`, any
+other value is merged recursively (or, for non-object values, inserted after cloning so the result
+never shares structure with `patch`). This reuses the `isObject` and `deepClone` helpers already in
+the file (used by `applyPatch`), keeping the merge logic in a small private `mergeInto` helper.
+
+Following the project's test-first directive, added tests for every worked example in RFC 7386
+appendix A before writing the implementation (confirmed red: `applyMergePatch` did not exist yet),
+then implemented until they passed. Also added tests, parallel to existing `applyPatch` coverage,
+that `applyMergePatch` does not mutate `target` or `patch`, and that its result shares no mutable
+structure with either (so a later mutation of the result cannot leak back into the caller's inputs).
+
+Verified `src/index.ts` still compiles with zero errors under the existing strict `tsconfig.json`
+(temporary, uncommitted `typescript` install, same approach as dl-001), since `tsc` cannot currently
+typecheck the test file. `node --test` passes 60/60 (41 pre-existing + 19 new tests covering every
+RFC 7386 appendix A example plus the mutation/aliasing guarantees).
+
+Documented the new function in `README.md`, alongside the existing JSON Pointer and JSON Patch
+sections.
+
+## Follow-ups
+
+- dl-001's decision to defer `tsc` typechecking of test files still applies; this change was
+  verified the same way.
+- No new decision-log was needed: RFC 7386's algorithm is unambiguous, and the cloning strategy
+  follows the precedent already set by dl-002/dl-003 for `applyPatch` (never let the result of an
+  `apply*` function share mutable structure with either its document/target or its patch argument).
